@meta
id: 09-07
title: Drill — three words into one
chapter: 09
type: drill
skills: op-counts
par: 14
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: select the phrase, type over it
open: status.md

@brief
Eight times: replace `three failed attempts` with `5`, save, quit.

The point is the count landing in the right place without you thinking
about it.

@keys
| `4w` | four words forward |
| `c3w` | change three words |

@hints
Get to `three` first, then change the three words that follow the cursor.
--
`4w` then `c3w`, type `5`, `Esc`.

@solution
    4w c3w 5 <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Eleven keystrokes. Notice that the final `w` of `c3w` stops at the full
stop rather than swallowing it: the third `w` motion lands on the `.`,
which is where the change ends. You did not have to think about the
punctuation, and you should not start.

By the last rep you want `c3w` to be one unit in your head, the way `c`
and `w` already are. That is how the grammar pays off — not by giving you
clever edits, but by making ordinary ones cost nothing to compose.

@start status.md
The retry limit is three failed attempts.

@expected status.md
The retry limit is 5.
